Library mode for the C emitter (bend2/comp.ts, bend2/main.ts):
`bend P.bend -o P.c --lib name1,name2` writes C with no main() that
exports the named defs to a host (docs/decisions/0001 of bend-wasm: state
that lives across calls from a game loop, natively and in wasm).

- bend_init() sets the runtime up as main does before running main (the
  corpus, the allocator, one worker and its Term stack); each named def
  is a C function bend_def_<name>; bend_dispose(handle) drops a value;
  bend_heap_words() is the heap high-water mark in words (the pages the
  allocator took from the corpus), a debug export.
- Which defs: the ones js_lib exports to JS (js_outs, shared with it).
  U32 and F32 cross as uint32_t and float, a Nat (up to 2^48-1) as
  uint64_t; any other closure-free datatype crosses as an opaque u64
  handle: a root-table slot (low 32 bits) and its generation (high 32).
  The slot holds the value's words in the def's own layout and a type id,
  so a handle of another type, or a consumed or disposed one, fails with
  a message and exit 1. Other types (functions, open types) stop the
  emitter with an error.
- A handle passed as a +parameter is borrowed: the host keeps it with
  term_keep (its type is made hot, so the def takes a shared value), or
  passes it as is when the emitter's lend analysis found the def only
  reads it. Any other handle argument is consumed (affine use): its slot
  dies and its generation moves on.
- The host lends every argument (fl.lend), so an exported def borrows
  what it does not own; the roots of the table stay valid because the
  corpus is mapped once and never reset.
- Ordinary programs: io_loop and main moved into RUN_LOOP and RUN_MAIN
  unchanged, so their emitted C is byte-identical; only --lib replaces them.

Retired by: an upstream C library or FFI output mode
(https://github.com/bendlang/bend/issues/1598).

